From: Cubie A7S robot project
Subject: [PATCH] img-bxm: run the gpu at full clock, apply requests made while it's off

Without imagination's own governor (not in the allwinner ddk drop) the
driver registers simple_ondemand. It only ever got zero load from this
driver and kept the gpu at 400MHz under full load, logging "dvfs failed
with (-11)" twice a second. Register with performance: full clock when
busy, the gpu power-gates when idle, and the thermal cooling device caps
the clock when it gets hot.

devfreq_target() also ignored any request while the gpu was power-gated
(dvfs state OFF) and reported the old clock as accepted. The gpu gates
between bursts of work, so a thermal cap released while it was off left it
at 400MHz until the next reboot. With the gpu off there's no firmware to
coordinate with: under the power lock, set voltage and clock in the usual
order and record the new speed, which the driver hands to the firmware at
power up.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff -ru a/bsp/modules/gpu/img-bxm/linux/rogue_km/services/server/env/linux/pvr_dvfs_device.c b/bsp/modules/gpu/img-bxm/linux/rogue_km/services/server/env/linux/pvr_dvfs_device.c
--- a/bsp/modules/gpu/img-bxm/linux/rogue_km/services/server/env/linux/pvr_dvfs_device.c	2026-11-11 22:04:48.370354292 +0100
+++ b/bsp/modules/gpu/img-bxm/linux/rogue_km/services/server/env/linux/pvr_dvfs_device.c	2026-11-11 22:05:35.584686138 +0100
@@ -132,6 +132,40 @@
 	}
 
 	psRGXTimingInfo = psRGXData->psRGXTimingInfo;
+
+	/* gpu off: no firmware to sync with, set the clock now (dropping the request kept the old one) */
+	if (psDVFSDevice->eState == PVR_DVFS_STATE_OFF &&
+	    PVRSRVPowerLock(psDeviceNode) == PVRSRV_OK)
+	{
+		if (psDVFSDevice->eState == PVR_DVFS_STATE_OFF)
+		{
+			opp = devfreq_recommended_opp(dev, requested_freq, flags);
+			if (IS_ERR(opp))
+			{
+				PVRSRVPowerUnlock(psDeviceNode);
+				return PTR_ERR(opp);
+			}
+			ui32Freq = dev_pm_opp_get_freq(opp);
+			ui32Volt = dev_pm_opp_get_voltage(opp);
+			dev_pm_opp_put(opp);
+
+			ui32CurFreq = psRGXTimingInfo->ui32CoreClockSpeed;
+			if (ui32Freq > ui32CurFreq)
+				psDVFSDeviceCfg->pfnSetVoltage(psDeviceNode->psDevConfig->hSysData, ui32Volt);
+			if (ui32Freq != ui32CurFreq)
+				psDVFSDeviceCfg->pfnSetFrequency(psDeviceNode->psDevConfig->hSysData, ui32Freq);
+			if (ui32Freq < ui32CurFreq)
+				psDVFSDeviceCfg->pfnSetVoltage(psDeviceNode->psDevConfig->hSysData, ui32Volt);
+			psRGXTimingInfo->ui32CoreClockSpeed = ui32Freq;
+
+			PVRSRVPowerUnlock(psDeviceNode);
+			*requested_freq = ui32Freq;
+			return 0;
+		}
+		/* it powered up meanwhile, take the normal path */
+		PVRSRVPowerUnlock(psDeviceNode);
+	}
+
 	if (psDVFSDevice->eState != PVR_DVFS_STATE_READY)
 	{
 		*requested_freq = psRGXTimingInfo->ui32CoreClockSpeed;
@@ -1180,7 +1214,8 @@
 													  "pvr_balanced",
 													  &psDVFSDevice->data);
 #else
-													  "simple_ondemand",
+													  /* full clock when busy, it power-gates when idle and thermal caps it; simple_ondemand saw zero load, stuck at 400MHz */
+													  "performance",
 													  &psDVFSDevice->data);
 #endif
 
